Absolutely. These are **classic string/pattern matching algorithms**. They solve the problem:

> Given a large text `T` of length `n`, find occurrences of a pattern `P` of length `m`.

For example:

```text
Text:    "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"
Pattern: "brown"
```

We want to find where `"brown"` occurs.

The important difference between these algorithms is **how they avoid unnecessary character comparisons**.

---

# 1. First understand the problem

Suppose:

```text
T = "AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAB"
P = "AAAAB"
```

A naive algorithm tries the pattern at every position:

```text
AAAAA...
AAAAA...
AAAAA...
AAAAA...
```

This can result in roughly:

[
O(nm)
]

comparisons.

The algorithms you listed try to do much better.

---

# 2. KMP — Knuth-Morris-Pratt

## Main idea

KMP says:

> When a mismatch happens, don't start comparing from the beginning of the pattern again. Use information about the pattern that you already matched.

Complexity:

[
\boxed{O(n+m)}
]

This is guaranteed.

---

## Example

Pattern:

```text
P = ABABC
```

We need to construct an auxiliary array called:

```text
LPS
```

LPS = **Longest Proper Prefix which is also a Suffix**.

For:

```text
A B A B C
```

we get:

```text
index:  0 1 2 3 4
P:      A B A B C
LPS:    0 0 1 2 0
```

Why?

For substring:

```text
ABA
```

the longest prefix that is also a suffix is:

```text
A
```

so:

```text
LPS[2] = 1
```

For:

```text
ABAB
```

prefix:

```text
AB
```

suffix:

```text
AB
```

therefore:

```text
LPS[3] = 2
```

---

## How KMP uses LPS

Suppose:

```text
Text:
ABABABABC

Pattern:
ABABC
```

We match:

```text
ABAB
```

and then get:

```text
Text:    A B A B A B A B C
Pattern: A B A B C
                 ↑
              mismatch
```

Naively we'd restart pattern matching from:

```text
A
```

But KMP knows:

```text
ABAB
```

has a suffix:

```text
AB
```

that is also a prefix of the pattern.

So instead of going back to zero:

```text
j = LPS[j-1]
```

which gives:

```text
j = 2
```

We continue from:

```text
AB
```

---

## Why KMP is O(n+m)

The text pointer never moves backward.

Pattern pointer can move backward using LPS, but overall the amount of movement is bounded.

Therefore:

[
O(n)+O(m)
]

=

[
\boxed{O(n+m)}
]

---

## When to use KMP

Good when:

* you have one pattern
* you need guaranteed linear time
* worst-case performance matters
* text/pattern can contain repetitive structures

Example:

```text
DNA sequence matching
log searching
compiler parsing
text searching
```

---

# 3. Boyer-Moore

Boyer-Moore takes a very different approach.

Instead of comparing:

```text
left → right
```

it compares:

```text
right → left
```

This allows it to **skip large portions of the text**.

That's the important idea.

---

## Example

Pattern:

```text
P = EXAMPLE
```

Suppose text contains:

```text
THIS IS A SIMPLE EXAMPLE TEXT
```

Boyer-Moore aligns the pattern and starts comparing from:

```text
E X A M P L E
            ↑
```

If the character doesn't match, it asks:

> Can I safely shift the pattern several positions instead of moving just one?

Often the answer is yes.

---

# 4. Boyer-Moore has two major heuristics

## 4.1 Bad-character rule

Suppose:

```text
Pattern = EXAMPLE
```

and we compare from right to left.

Imagine we get:

```text
EXAMPLE
     X
     ↑
```

and the text character doesn't match.

We look at the mismatching text character.

If that character doesn't occur anywhere in the pattern, we can skip the entire pattern.

For example:

```text
Pattern = ABCD
Text    = XXXXABCD
             ↑
```

If the mismatch character is `X`, and:

```text
X ∉ ABCD
```

then there is no reason for the pattern to start at any of the next `m-1` positions.

We can jump:

```text
ABCD
    ↓
        ABCD
```

---

# 5. Good-suffix rule

This is the second major Boyer-Moore optimization.

Suppose we matched a suffix:

```text
...ABC
```

and then got a mismatch.

Boyer-Moore asks:

> Does this matched suffix occur somewhere else in the pattern?

If yes, shift the pattern so that occurrence lines up.

This can produce very large jumps.

---

# 6. Why Boyer-Moore is called "sublinear"

You will often see:

[
\boxed{\text{Boyer-Moore has sublinear average behavior}}
]

This means that in many practical cases, it examines **fewer than `n` characters on average**.

It can skip large regions.

But be careful:

> "Sublinear" does NOT mean the worst-case complexity is always sublinear.

The original/general Boyer-Moore has complicated worst-case behavior depending on the implementation/heuristics.

Its big practical advantage is:

[
\boxed{\text{large skips}}
]

---

# 7. BM-Horspool

Boyer-Moore-Horspool is a simplified version of Boyer-Moore.

Instead of using both:

* bad character
* good suffix

it primarily uses the **bad-character shift**.

And it makes the implementation much simpler.

---

## How it works

Suppose:

```text
Pattern = ABCDE
```

Horspool builds a shift table.

For every character except the last:

```text
A → 4
B → 3
C → 2
D → 1
```

Characters not in the pattern:

```text
anything else → 5
```

The last character is special because after a mismatch, we inspect the character aligned with the **last pattern position** and determine how far to shift.

---

## Example

```text
Pattern = TEST
```

Approximate shift table:

```text
T → 3
E → 2
S → 1
other → 4
```

So if the text character aligned with the final `T` is:

```text
X
```

then:

```text
X not in pattern
```

so we can shift by:

```text
4
```

instead of:

```text
1
```

---

## Why Horspool is popular

Because it gives much of the practical speed of Boyer-Moore with a much simpler implementation.

Good for:

```text
single-pattern searching
large text
natural language
practical search utilities
```

---

# 8. Rabin-Karp

Rabin-Karp takes a completely different approach.

Instead of comparing strings character-by-character immediately, it uses a:

[
\boxed{\text{rolling hash}}
]

---

## Basic idea

Suppose:

```text
Pattern = ABC
```

Calculate:

```text
hash("ABC")
```

Now take a text window:

```text
Text = XYZABCDE
```

Look at windows of length 3:

```text
XYZ
YZA
ZAB
ABC
BCD
CDE
```

Instead of comparing every string directly, calculate:

```text
hash(XYZ)
hash(YZA)
hash(ZAB)
hash(ABC)
...
```

If:

```text
hash(window) == hash(pattern)
```

then perform an actual character comparison.

Why?

Because two different strings can have the same hash.

That's called a:

[
\boxed{\text{hash collision}}
]

---

# 9. Rolling hash

The clever part is that we don't calculate each hash from scratch.

Suppose:

```text
ABC
```

has a hash:

[
H(ABC)
]

Now move the window:

```text
BCD
```

We remove:

```text
A
```

and add:

```text
D
```

So we update the hash efficiently.

Conceptually:

[
H_{\text{new}}
==============

(H_{\text{old}}-\text{removed contribution})\times base
+\text{new character}
]

Therefore each window can be processed in approximately:

[
O(1)
]

hash-update time.

---

# 10. Rabin-Karp complexity

Average:

[
\boxed{O(n+m)}
]

With a good hash function, usually very efficient.

Worst case can degrade because of many hash collisions:

[
O(nm)
]

---

## Where Rabin-Karp shines

Especially useful when you need to search for:

**multiple patterns of the same length.**

For example:

```text
Patterns:
cat
dog
fox
cow
```

You can hash all patterns and then scan the text using rolling hashes.

---

# 11. Aho-Corasick

This is one of the most important ones in your list.

Especially because you mentioned:

> gazetteer/tag matching

Aho-Corasick is specifically designed for:

[
\boxed{\text{multiple pattern matching}}
]

Suppose you have:

```text
Patterns:
he
she
his
hers
```

and text:

```text
ushers
```

We want to find all patterns simultaneously.

---

# 12. Aho-Corasick uses a Trie

First build a Trie.

For:

```text
he
she
his
hers
```

conceptually:

```text
             root
            /    \
           h      s
           |      |
           e      h
          /       |
         r        e
         |       / \
         s      ?   ...
```

More accurately, shared prefixes are represented by shared trie paths.

For example:

```text
he
hers
```

share:

```text
h → e
```

So we don't store them independently.

---

# 13. Failure links

The most important part of Aho-Corasick is the:

[
\boxed{\text{failure link}}
]

Suppose we're matching:

```text
she
```

and we've reached:

```text
sh
```

but the next character doesn't continue that path.

Instead of starting over from the root, the failure link says:

> Where is the longest suffix of what I've matched that is also a valid Trie prefix?

This is conceptually similar to KMP's fallback idea.

---

# 14. Output links

Aho-Corasick also needs to know:

> Which patterns end at this state?

For example, while matching:

```text
she
```

we find:

```text
she
```

but we also know:

```text
he
```

is a suffix of:

```text
she
```

Therefore both can be reported.

This is why Aho-Corasick is excellent for dictionaries/gazetteers.

---

# 15. Aho-Corasick example

Patterns:

```text
cat
car
cart
dog
```

Text:

```text
I have a cat and a cart and a dog.
```

The automaton scans the text once.

It can report:

```text
cat
car
cart
dog
```

without running four separate searches.

---

# 16. Complexity of Aho-Corasick

Let:

* `n` = text length
* total pattern length = `M`
* `z` = number of matches

Building the automaton:

[
O(M)
]

roughly, depending on alphabet/transition representation.

Searching:

[
\boxed{O(n+z)}
]

This is the complexity you quoted.

More precisely, the output/reporting cost is proportional to the number of matches.

This is why it is ideal for:

```text
gazetteers
named entity dictionaries
keyword detection
tag matching
profanity filters
intrusion signatures
DNA motifs
dictionary matching
```

---

# 17. Why Aho-Corasick beats running KMP repeatedly

Suppose you have:

```text
1,000,000 patterns
```

and a text of:

```text
10 MB
```

Running KMP separately:

```text
KMP(pattern1)
KMP(pattern2)
KMP(pattern3)
...
```

would be expensive.

Aho-Corasick builds one automaton containing all patterns:

```text
                 ┌── pattern 1
                 │
Text → Automaton ├── pattern 2
                 │
                 ├── pattern 3
                 │
                 └── pattern N
```

Then scans the text once.

---

# 18. Commentz-Walter

Commentz-Walter is basically an interesting combination of ideas from:

* Boyer-Moore
* Aho-Corasick

It is designed for:

[
\boxed{\text{multiple-pattern searching}}
]

while trying to preserve the skipping behavior of Boyer-Moore.

---

## The problem

Aho-Corasick:

```text
scan text left → right
```

and guarantees:

[
O(n+\text{matches})
]

But sometimes you want to **skip portions of the text** like Boyer-Moore.

Commentz-Walter tries to do this.

---

## Conceptually

Aho-Corasick gives us:

```text
Trie
+
failure links
```

Boyer-Moore gives us:

```text
right-to-left comparison
+
large shifts
```

Commentz-Walter combines these concepts.

So:

```text
Multiple patterns
        ↓
Aho-Corasick-like structure
        +
Boyer-Moore-like skipping
        ↓
Commentz-Walter
```

It is useful when you have many patterns and want practical skipping rather than purely automaton-based scanning.

---

# 19. Bitap / Shift-Or

Bitap is very different.

It uses:

[
\boxed{\text{bit-parallelism}}
]

Instead of representing pattern matching with normal integers/arrays, it represents the matching state using **bits** in a machine word.

For short patterns this can be extremely fast.

---

# 20. Basic Bitap idea

Suppose:

```text
Pattern = ABC
```

We have three pattern positions:

```text
A B C
0 1 2
```

Represent the state as bits:

```text
001
010
100
```

As characters arrive, bit operations update the matching state.

Operations such as:

```text
AND
OR
SHIFT
```

can update many states simultaneously.

---

# 21. Why is that powerful?

Normally you might update:

```text
state[0]
state[1]
state[2]
state[3]
...
```

one at a time.

Bit-parallel algorithms can update many states using a **single CPU instruction**.

For example, a 64-bit integer can conceptually hold:

```text
64 matching states
```

and:

```text
x << 1
```

moves all of them simultaneously.

Therefore Bitap can be extremely fast for short patterns.

---

# 22. Bitap pattern length limitation

Classic Bitap is constrained by machine word size.

For example, with a 64-bit word, the simple implementation naturally handles patterns up to roughly:

```text
64 characters
```

Longer patterns require:

```text
multiple machine words
```

or another implementation.

Modern implementations can use wider SIMD/vector registers as well.

---

# 23. Bitap can also support approximate matching

This is particularly interesting.

Bitap can be extended to handle:

```text
exact matching
```

and:

```text
fuzzy matching
```

such as:

```text
Pattern = kitten
Text    = kittan
```

with one edit/substitution.

This makes Bitap useful for:

```text
spell checking
fuzzy search
short pattern matching
approximate string matching
```

---

# 24. Now compare all of them

| Algorithm           | Patterns | Main idea                         | Typical complexity             |
| ------------------- | -------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| **KMP**             | Single   | LPS/failure information           | `O(n+m)`                       |
| **Boyer-Moore**     | Single   | Right-to-left + large skips       | Often sublinear average        |
| **BM-Horspool**     | Single   | Simplified bad-character skipping | Very fast practical average    |
| **Rabin-Karp**      | One/many | Rolling hash                      | Avg. `O(n+m)`, worst `O(nm)`   |
| **Aho-Corasick**    | Many     | Trie + failure links              | `O(n + matches)`               |
| **Commentz-Walter** | Many     | Aho-Corasick + BM-style skipping  | Practical multi-pattern search |
| **Bitap/Shift-Or**  | Single   | Bit-parallel matching             | Very fast for short patterns   |

---

# 25. The key conceptual difference

You can organize them like this:

```text
                 STRING MATCHING
                       │
          ┌────────────┴────────────┐
          │                         │
      SINGLE PATTERN           MULTIPLE PATTERNS
          │                         │
     ┌────┼─────┐             ┌─────┴─────┐
     │    │     │             │           │
    KMP   BM   Bitap       Aho-Corasick  Commentz-Walter
          │
       Horspool
          
          │
       Rabin-Karp
```

But Rabin-Karp is somewhat special because it naturally extends to multiple patterns, especially patterns of equal length.

---

# 26. KMP vs Boyer-Moore

This is a very important distinction.

### KMP

Thinks:

> "I've already matched this prefix, so I know where I can continue."

```text
LEFT → RIGHT
```

Uses:

```text
LPS
```

Guarantees:

[
O(n+m)
]

---

### Boyer-Moore

Thinks:

> "This mismatch tells me I can move the pattern far to the right."

```text
RIGHT → LEFT
```

Uses:

```text
bad character
good suffix
```

Often extremely fast in real text.

---

# 27. KMP vs Aho-Corasick

This distinction is even more important for search engines.

### KMP

One pattern:

```text
"machine learning"
```

Search:

```text
document → KMP
```

---

### Aho-Corasick

Many patterns:

```text
"machine learning"
"deep learning"
"neural network"
"transformer"
"attention"
"retrieval"
...
```

Build:

```text
                    Trie
                     +
               failure links
                     ↓
                  automaton
                     ↓
                   text
                     ↓
            all matching patterns
```

This is why Aho-Corasick is frequently used for **gazetteer/tag matching**.

---

# 28. Why this matters for information retrieval

Since you're studying indexing/search, these algorithms appear at different levels.

Imagine you have:

```text
100 million documents
```

and a query:

```text
"information retrieval"
```

You usually **don't scan every document character-by-character**.

Instead, an inverted index tells you:

```text
information → [doc1, doc4, doc8, doc100...]
retrieval   → [doc1, doc3, doc8, doc90...]
```

Then you intersect posting lists.

String matching becomes useful in other components:

```text
                 Search System
                      │
        ┌─────────────┼──────────────┐
        ↓             ↓              ↓
   Tokenization    Dictionary     Query parsing
                       │
                       ↓
               Aho-Corasick
                       │
                 gazetteer
                       │
                       ↓
            "New York", "Mumbai",
            "Google", "OpenAI", ...
```

---

# 29. Example: Gazetteer matching

Suppose your gazetteer contains:

```text
Mumbai
New York
New Delhi
Delhi
San Francisco
Google
Microsoft
OpenAI
```

Input:

```text
"OpenAI opened a new office in New Delhi."
```

Aho-Corasick can identify:

```text
OpenAI
New Delhi
Delhi
```

depending on whether overlapping matches are reported.

You could then perform:

```text
text
 ↓
Aho-Corasick
 ↓
matched spans
 ↓
entity/tag IDs
 ↓
indexing / annotation
```

This is much more efficient than running a separate string search for every gazetteer entry.

---

# 30. One important subtlety: `O(n + matches)`

When you see:

[
O(n+\text{matches})
]

don't interpret it as simply "Aho-Corasick is always O(n)."

Suppose:

```text
Text = aaaaaaaaaa...
Patterns =
a
aa
aaa
aaaa
...
```

There can be an enormous number of matches.

For example:

```text
aaaaaaaa

a
aa
aaa
aaaa
...
```

Many patterns match at the same position.

So if you actually need to **output every match**, the output itself takes time.

That's why:

[
O(n+\text{matches})
]

is the correct way to express the search cost.

---

# 31. A useful mental model

Remember them this way:

### KMP

> **Don't restart unnecessarily.**

```text
LPS
```

### Boyer-Moore

> **Skip as far as possible.**

```text
right → left
```

### Horspool

> **Simplified Boyer-Moore.**

```text
bad-character shift
```

### Rabin-Karp

> **Compare fingerprints first.**

```text
rolling hash
```

### Aho-Corasick

> **Search an entire dictionary simultaneously.**

```text
Trie + failure links
```

### Commentz-Walter

> **Aho-Corasick + Boyer-Moore-style skipping.**

### Bitap

> **Turn matching into bit operations.**

```text
SHIFT + AND + OR
```

---

# 32. Which one should you choose?

A practical decision tree:

```text
Do I have one pattern?
        │
       YES
        │
        ├── Need guaranteed O(n+m)?
        │        ↓
        │       KMP
        │
        ├── Want very fast practical search?
        │        ↓
        │    Boyer-Moore /
        │    Horspool
        │
        ├── Pattern is short?
        │        ↓
        │      Bitap
        │
        └── Hashing/multiple equal-length patterns?
                 ↓
             Rabin-Karp


Do I have MANY patterns?
        │
       YES
        │
        ├── Dictionary/gazetteer/tag matching?
        │        ↓
        │   Aho-Corasick
        │
        └── Want multi-pattern BM-style skipping?
                 ↓
           Commentz-Walter
```

The **big picture** is that KMP avoids restarting, Boyer-Moore skips ahead, Rabin-Karp avoids direct comparisons using hashes, Aho-Corasick turns many patterns into one automaton, Commentz-Walter combines multi-pattern matching with skipping, and Bitap turns the matching state into CPU-friendly bit operations.







Absolutely. This whole section is about **finding strings that are similar even when they are not exactly equal**—for example:

* `"cat"` → `"cut"`
* `"receive"` → `"recieve"`
* `"colour"` → `"color"`
* `"jon"` → `"john"`

This is extremely important in **search engines, spell correction, OCR correction, name matching, entity resolution, and fuzzy search**.

I'll build it from the basic idea → algorithms → data structures → how Lucene/SymSpell/Norvig actually use them.

---

# 1. What is Edit Distance?

Suppose we have two strings:

```text
A = kitten
B = sitting
```

We want to know:

> How many operations are required to transform `kitten` into `sitting`?

Typical edit operations are:

1. **Insertion** — insert a character
2. **Deletion** — delete a character
3. **Substitution** — replace one character with another
4. **Transposition** — swap two adjacent characters

For example:

```text
cat → cut
```

Only one substitution:

```text
cat
 ↓
cut
```

So:

```text
distance(cat, cut) = 1
```

---

# 2. Levenshtein Distance

The most fundamental edit-distance algorithm is **Levenshtein distance**.

It allows:

* insertion
* deletion
* substitution

but **not transposition**.

## Example

```text
kitten
sitting
```

One possible transformation:

```text
kitten
sitten    substitute k → s
sittin    substitute e → i
sitting   insert g
```

Therefore:

```text
distance = 3
```

---

# 3. Dynamic Programming for Levenshtein

This is one of the most important concepts.

Let:

```text
A = a1 a2 ... am
B = b1 b2 ... bn
```

Define:

[
D[i][j]
]

as:

> minimum edit distance between the first `i` characters of A and first `j` characters of B.

So:

```text
D[i][j] = distance(A[:i], B[:j])
```

---

## Base cases

Transforming a string into an empty string requires deletions.

Therefore:

[
D[i][0] = i
]

Similarly, creating a string from an empty string requires insertions:

[
D[0][j] = j
]

Example:

```text
      ""  s  i  t  t  i  n  g
""     0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7
k      1
i      2
t      3
t      4
e      5
n      6
```

---

# 4. Levenshtein Recurrence

For each pair of characters:

```text
A[i-1]
B[j-1]
```

If they are equal:

[
D[i][j] = D[i-1][j-1]
]

because no operation is necessary.

If they are different:

[
D[i][j]
=======

1+\min
\begin{cases}
D[i-1][j] & \text{deletion}\
D[i][j-1] & \text{insertion}\
D[i-1][j-1] & \text{substitution}
\end{cases}
]

The `1` means:

> We just performed one edit.

---

# 5. Why Three Directions?

Look at the DP matrix:

```text
             B
          j-1   j
           ↖   ←
        ↑
        i-1
```

There are three possibilities.

### Deletion

```text
A[i-1] removed
```

Move:

```text
D[i-1][j]
```

### Insertion

Insert `B[j-1]`.

Move:

```text
D[i][j-1]
```

### Substitution

Replace:

```text
A[i-1] → B[j-1]
```

Move:

```text
D[i-1][j-1]
```

---

# 6. Complexity

For strings of lengths `m` and `n`:

Number of DP cells:

[
(m+1)(n+1)
]

Therefore:

[
Time = O(mn)
]

and standard full-matrix space:

[
Space = O(mn)
]

But because each row only depends on the previous row, we can reduce space to:

[
O(\min(m,n))
]

This is very important in real implementations.

---

# 7. Damerau–Levenshtein Distance

Levenshtein doesn't allow transposition.

Consider:

```text
form
from
```

The letters:

```text
o r
```

have been swapped:

```text
fo rm
fr om
```

A transposition is:

```text
or → ro
```

Damerau–Levenshtein adds:

> **Adjacent character transposition**

So:

```text
form → from
```

has distance:

[
1
]

instead of requiring two substitutions.

---

# 8. Damerau–Levenshtein Operations

It supports:

```text
Insertion
Deletion
Substitution
Transposition
```

For example:

```text
hte → the
```

The first two characters are swapped:

```text
ht → th
```

So transposition handles common typing errors very naturally.

This is why it can be useful for:

* spell checking
* OCR
* keyboard mistakes
* names
* search queries

---

# 9. Why Transposition Matters So Much

Human typing errors often look like:

```text
teh → the
recieve → receive
form → from
```

Without transposition, these may require more edits than they intuitively should.

For example:

```text
teh
the
```

A transposition:

```text
teh
 ↓
the
```

gives:

[
distance = 1
]

---

# 10. Important Distinction: Optimal String Alignment vs True Damerau–Levenshtein

You'll sometimes encounter two variants.

### Optimal String Alignment (OSA)

Allows adjacent transposition, but generally doesn't allow characters involved in transpositions to be reused arbitrarily.

### True Damerau–Levenshtein

Allows more general sequences of edits and therefore can produce a smaller distance in certain cases.

For search/spell-correction discussions, people sometimes loosely say "Damerau–Levenshtein" while actually implementing OSA.

That's an implementation detail worth remembering.

---

# 11. Needleman–Wunsch

Now we move from ordinary edit distance to **sequence alignment**.

Needleman–Wunsch is designed for:

> **Global alignment**

That means:

> Align the entire first sequence against the entire second sequence.

It was originally developed for biological sequence comparison.

For example:

```text
ACCGT
ACGGT
```

We align:

```text
A C C G T
| |   | |
A C G G T
```

The algorithm gives scores rather than simply counting edits.

---

# 12. Why Not Just Levenshtein?

Levenshtein basically says:

```text
match       = 0 cost
substitution = 1 cost
insertion   = 1 cost
deletion    = 1 cost
```

Needleman–Wunsch is more general.

You define:

```text
match score
mismatch penalty
gap penalty
```

For example:

```text
match       +2
mismatch    -1
gap         -2
```

So biologically meaningful substitutions can have different costs.

---

# 13. Needleman–Wunsch DP

Let:

[
F[i][j]
]

represent the best alignment score between:

```text
A[:i]
B[:j]
```

Then:

[
F[i][j]
=======

\max
\begin{cases}
F[i-1][j-1]+score(A_i,B_j)\
F[i-1][j]-gap\
F[i][j-1]-gap
\end{cases}
]

Three possibilities:

```text
↖ match/mismatch

↑ deletion/gap

← insertion/gap
```

---

# 14. Global vs Local Alignment

This distinction is **very important**.

### Needleman–Wunsch

Global:

```text
AAAACTGG
||||||||
AAAACTGG
```

It tries to align the **whole sequences**.

### Smith–Waterman

Local:

```text
AAAACTGGTTAC
     |||||
     CTGGT
```

It searches for the **best matching region**.

---

# 15. Smith–Waterman

Smith–Waterman is essentially a **local alignment algorithm**.

Instead of asking:

> How well do these entire strings match?

it asks:

> What is the best matching substring/region between these strings?

This is useful when:

```text
A = "thequickbrownfox"
B = "xxxquickbrownxxx"
```

There is a strong local match:

```text
quickbrown
```

even though the entire strings are different.

---

# 16. The Key Smith–Waterman Difference

The recurrence includes:

[
0
]

For example:

[
F[i][j]
=======

\max
\begin{cases}
0\
F[i-1][j-1]+score\
F[i-1][j]-gap\
F[i][j-1]-gap
\end{cases}
]

Why?

Because if all possible alignments are bad, we can simply **start a new alignment here**.

That's what makes it local.

---

# 17. Needleman–Wunsch vs Smith–Waterman

| Algorithm        | Alignment            | Main idea                 |
| ---------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Needleman–Wunsch | Global               | Align entire sequences    |
| Smith–Waterman   | Local                | Find best matching region |
| Levenshtein      | Global edit distance | Minimum edits             |

Think:

```text
Needleman-Wunsch
A ----------------
B ----------------
      whole thing

Smith-Waterman
A ------------------------
       ███████
B ---- ███████ -----------
       best region
```

---

# 18. Hamming Distance

Hamming distance is much simpler.

It only works when the strings have **the same length**.

It counts positions where characters differ.

Example:

```text
A = 1011101
B = 1001001
```

Compare:

```text
1 = 1
0 = 0
1 ≠ 0
1 = 1
1 ≠ 0
0 = 0
1 = 1
```

So:

[
H(A,B)=2
]

---

# 19. Hamming vs Levenshtein

Suppose:

```text
cat
cut
```

Both have length 3.

Hamming:

```text
c = c
a ≠ u
t = t
```

Distance:

```text
1
```

Levenshtein:

```text
1 substitution
```

Also:

```text
cat
cats
```

Hamming:

> Not defined because lengths differ.

Levenshtein:

```text
1 insertion
```

Therefore:

> **Hamming only handles substitutions at fixed positions.**

---

# 20. Jaro Similarity

Jaro is especially useful for:

* names
* short strings
* duplicate detection
* entity matching

Examples:

```text
MARTHA
MARHTA
```

The characters are mostly the same but some are rearranged.

Jaro looks at:

1. matching characters
2. character order
3. lengths

The basic Jaro similarity is:

[
J =
\frac{1}{3}
\left(
\frac{m}{|s_1|}
+
\frac{m}{|s_2|}
+
\frac{m-t}{m}
\right)
]

where:

* `m` = number of matching characters
* `t` = number of transpositions / half-transpositions

The result is between:

```text
0 → completely different
1 → identical
```

---

# 21. What Does "Matching Character" Mean?

Jaro doesn't simply compare:

```text
s1[i] == s2[i]
```

It allows a character to match within a certain **window**.

The matching window is approximately:

[
\left\lfloor
\frac{\max(|s_1|,|s_2|)}{2}
\right\rfloor-1
]

This allows characters to move around somewhat.

That's why Jaro works well for names.

---

# 22. Jaro–Winkler

Jaro-Winkler adds a very useful idea:

> Give extra weight to a common prefix.

For example:

```text
MARTHA
MARHTA
```

Both start with:

```text
MAR
```

A common prefix is strong evidence that the strings represent the same name.

Jaro-Winkler:

[
JW = J + l p(1-J)
]

where:

* `J` = Jaro similarity
* `l` = common prefix length
* `p` = scaling factor

Usually:

```text
p = 0.1
```

and `l` is capped, commonly at 4.

---

# 23. Why Jaro-Winkler Is Good for Names

Consider:

```text
Jonathan
Jonathon
```

They share a long prefix:

```text
Jonath...
```

Jaro-Winkler boosts their similarity.

This is why it is commonly associated with:

```text
Name matching
Record linkage
Customer deduplication
Entity resolution
```

---

# 24. Comparing the Similarity Algorithms

A useful mental model:

```text
Hamming
   ↓
fixed-position comparison

Levenshtein
   ↓
insert/delete/substitute

Damerau-Levenshtein
   ↓
+ transposition

Jaro
   ↓
character matching + ordering

Jaro-Winkler
   ↓
Jaro + prefix bonus
```

---

# 25. Why Ordinary DP Becomes Expensive

Suppose you have:

```text
query = "internationalization"
dictionary = 10 million words
```

If you calculate Levenshtein distance against every dictionary word:

```text
query
   ↓
word 1 → DP
word 2 → DP
word 3 → DP
...
word 10,000,000 → DP
```

Each comparison costs roughly:

[
O(mn)
]

So this becomes expensive.

That's why we need **search structures and algorithms**.

This brings us to Ukkonen.

---

# 26. Ukkonen's Cutoff / Banded DP

Suppose you only care about:

```text
edit distance ≤ 2
```

Why calculate the entire DP matrix?

You don't need distances like:

```text
10
15
20
```

because you already know they're too large.

Ukkonen-style thresholding limits computation to the region around the main diagonal.

---

# 27. Why the Diagonal Matters

If two strings are very similar:

```text
A = kitten
B = sitten
```

their alignment is close to:

```text
k i t t e n
s i t t e n
```

The important DP cells are near:

```text
i ≈ j
```

Visualize:

```text
      j
    0 1 2 3 4 5 6
i 0 █ █
  1 █ █ █
  2   █ █ █
  3     █ █ █
  4       █ █ █
  5         █ █ █
  6           █
```

Instead of computing the whole matrix, calculate a **band**.

---

# 28. Banded DP

If allowed distance is `k`, calculate only cells satisfying approximately:

[
|i-j|\le k
]

For:

```text
k = 2
```

we calculate a band of width:

```text
2k + 1 = 5
```

instead of the entire matrix.

Therefore the work becomes approximately:

[
O(k\min(m,n))
]

when lengths are comparable.

You'll often see this simplified as:

[
O(kn)
]

for fixed/small `k`.

---

# 29. Why This Is Powerful

If:

```text
n = 1000
k = 2
```

Full DP:

[
1000 \times 1000 = 1,000,000
]

Banded:

[
1000 \times 5 = 5,000
]

Huge difference.

This is especially useful for:

```text
spell correction
OCR correction
fuzzy search
approximate matching
```

---

# 30. Levenshtein Automata

Now we're going from:

> "Calculate distance between two strings"

to:

> "Find all dictionary words within distance k of this query."

This is a major conceptual shift.

Suppose query:

```text
cat
```

and:

```text
k = 1
```

We want:

```text
cat
bat
cut
cart
at
cats
...
```

A **Levenshtein automaton** represents all strings whose edit distance from `"cat"` is ≤ 1.

Think of it as:

```text
             accepted
                ↓
             ┌─────┐
             │     │
start → states → ...
             │     │
             └─────┘
```

Any string that causes the automaton to end in an accepting state is within the allowed edit distance.

---

# 31. What Does the Automaton State Represent?

A state essentially represents:

> How far through the query we are and how many edits we've used.

For:

```text
query = cat
k = 1
```

the automaton tracks something like:

```text
position in "cat"
+
number of edits consumed
```

Conceptually:

```text
(q_position, edits_used)
```

For example:

```text
(0,0)
(1,0)
(1,1)
(2,0)
(2,1)
...
```

You don't need to think of it as literally storing every possible string.

It compactly represents the allowed edit states.

---

# 32. Why Automata Help Search

Suppose your dictionary is:

```text
cat
car
cart
cut
dog
apple
```

Instead of computing:

```text
distance(cat, cat)
distance(cat, car)
distance(cat, cart)
distance(cat, cut)
distance(cat, dog)
distance(cat, apple)
```

we can traverse the dictionary structure while simultaneously traversing the automaton.

This is where Lucene becomes interesting.

---

# 33. Lucene Fuzzy Search

Lucene supports fuzzy queries such as conceptually:

```text
roam~
```

meaning:

> Find terms approximately matching `"roam"`.

Lucene uses **Levenshtein automata** to efficiently enumerate terms within the allowed edit distance.

It doesn't simply calculate a full DP matrix against every dictionary term.

---

# 34. Lucene + FST

Lucene's term dictionary is represented using highly optimized structures, including **FSTs** in relevant parts of the term dictionary implementation.

FST:

> Finite State Transducer

Think of it as a compressed graph representing dictionary terms.

For example:

```text
             c
             |
             a
          /     \
         t       r
                 |
                 t
```

Conceptually:

```text
cat
car
cart
```

share prefixes.

Instead of storing:

```text
cat
car
cart
```

independently, the structure shares common paths.

---

# 35. Levenshtein Automaton + FST

This is the important idea:

```text
              Query
               │
               ▼
      Levenshtein Automaton
               │
               │ intersect
               ▼
          Dictionary FST
               │
               ▼
        matching dictionary
             terms
```

The intersection effectively finds:

> Dictionary terms accepted by the fuzzy automaton.

So Lucene doesn't need to compare the query with every term independently.

---

# 36. Why Lucene Caps Edit Distance at 2

This is an engineering tradeoff.

For fuzzy search:

```text
k = 1
```

is manageable.

```text
k = 2
```

is still practical.

But:

```text
k = 5
k = 10
```

causes the automaton/state space and candidate enumeration to grow significantly.

Also, high edit distances produce terrible search quality.

For example:

```text
query = apple
distance <= 5
```

almost everything becomes a candidate.

So Lucene historically caps fuzzy edit distance at:

[
k \le 2
]

This is both a **performance** and **relevance** decision.

---

# 37. BK-Trees

Another solution to fuzzy lookup is a **BK-tree**.

BK-tree means:

> Burkhard–Keller tree.

It works with a **metric distance**, such as Levenshtein distance.

The key mathematical property is the triangle inequality:

[
d(a,c) \le d(a,b)+d(b,c)
]

and:

[
d(a,c) \ge |d(a,b)-d(b,c)|
]

---

# 38. How BK-Tree Works

Suppose root is:

```text
book
```

We calculate distance from `"book"` to other words.

Suppose:

```text
back → 2
cook → 1
apple → 5
```

We organize children by distance:

```text
                book
             /   |    \
            1    2     5
            |    |     |
           cook back  apple
```

The edge number means:

> Distance from parent to child.

---

# 39. Searching a BK-Tree

Suppose query:

```text
boon
```

and allowed distance:

```text
k = 1
```

Calculate:

```text
d(boon, book) = 1
```

Because:

```text
boon
book
   ↑
```

Then we only need to explore child distances in a certain range.

If:

```text
d(query, node) = d
```

then candidates can only occur in:

[
[d-k,\ d+k]
]

because of the triangle inequality.

So if:

```text
d = 5
k = 1
```

only child edges:

```text
4, 5, 6
```

need to be explored.

That's the pruning mechanism.

---

# 40. BK-Tree Strength

It is simple:

```text
dictionary
     ↓
BK-tree
     ↓
query
     ↓
distance-based pruning
     ↓
near matches
```

No huge DP matrix for every dictionary word.

But performance depends heavily on:

* dataset
* language
* distance threshold
* distribution of words
* tree shape

---

# 41. SymSpell

SymSpell takes a very different approach.

Created by **Wolf Garbe**, it is based on the idea:

> Instead of generating every possible typo at query time, precompute deletions from dictionary words.

This is extremely clever.

---

# 42. Why Only Deletes?

Suppose dictionary word:

```text
hello
```

For edit distance `1`, deleting each character produces:

```text
ello
hllo
helo
helo
hell
```

Unique deletes:

```text
ello
hllo
helo
hell
```

Now suppose the user types:

```text
helo
```

Instead of generating:

```text
insertions
deletions
substitutions
transpositions
```

we can look up:

```text
helo
```

in a precomputed delete index.

It may map back to:

```text
hello
```

Then we verify the actual edit distance.

---

# 43. Symmetric Delete Idea

The key symmetry is surprising.

Suppose:

```text
dictionary word = hello
```

Delete:

```text
hello → helo
```

The misspelled word:

```text
helo
```

can therefore act as a key pointing to:

```text
hello
```

So instead of generating all possible corruptions of the query, we store deletion forms of dictionary words.

This dramatically reduces work.

---

# 44. Example

Dictionary:

```text
hello
help
helmet
```

For:

```text
hello
```

precompute deletes.

For distance 1:

```text
ello
hllo
helo
helo
hell
```

Store something like:

```text
"ello" → hello
"hllo" → hello
"helo" → hello
"hell" → hello
```

Now query:

```text
helo
```

can immediately retrieve:

```text
hello
```

---

# 45. Distance 2 and 3

If maximum edit distance is larger, generate deletion combinations.

For a word of length `L`, the number of ways to delete `d` characters is:

[
\binom{L}{d}
]

For example, with:

```text
L = 5
d = 3
```

we get:

[
\binom{5}{3}=10
]

unique positional combinations before deduplication.

Across **all deletion depths up to 3**:

[
\binom51+\binom52+\binom53
]

[
=5+10+10=25
]

That's the source of the statement:

> A 5-character word needs only 25 deletes for distance 3.

That's much smaller than generating all possible substitutions/insertions/transpositions.

---

# 46. But There's a Catch

SymSpell trades:

```text
runtime
```

for:

```text
memory
```

You must precompute and store deletion keys.

For large:

```text
dictionary
+
maximum distance
```

the number of deletion entries can become huge.

Therefore:

```text
small k → excellent
large k → space grows rapidly
```

---

# 47. SymSpell Search Pipeline

Conceptually:

```text
                 Dictionary
                     │
                     ▼
             generate deletes
                     │
                     ▼
              delete index
                     │
                     │
Query ───────────────┘
                     │
                     ▼
               candidates
                     │
                     ▼
          calculate actual distance
                     │
                     ▼
             rank candidates
```

Important:

> SymSpell uses deletes to generate candidates, but you still generally verify candidates using actual edit distance.

---

# 48. SymSpell vs BK-tree

|                     | SymSpell              | BK-tree            |
| ------------------- | --------------------- | ------------------ |
| Main idea           | Precompute deletes    | Metric tree        |
| Query speed         | Very fast             | Can be fast        |
| Memory              | High                  | Lower              |
| Preprocessing       | Significant           | Tree construction  |
| Large dictionary    | Excellent query speed | Depends            |
| Large edit distance | Space grows quickly   | Search grows       |
| Metric required     | No                    | Yes                |
| Typical use         | Spell correction      | Approximate lookup |

The famous performance claims such as "1M× faster than naive" or "~100× faster than BK-trees" are **benchmark-dependent claims**, not universal complexity guarantees.

That's important when evaluating such statements.

---

# 49. Norvig's Spell Corrector

Peter Norvig demonstrated a very simple spell-correction system.

The basic strategy:

1. Generate words within edit distance 1.
2. If no candidates, generate distance 2.
3. Choose the most frequent candidate.

For example:

```text
query = "speling"
```

Generate candidates:

```text
spelling
...
```

If `"spelling"` appears frequently in the corpus, select it.

---

# 50. Norvig's Candidate Generation

For a word:

```text
abc
```

distance-1 edits include:

### Deletes

```text
bc
ac
ab
```

### Transposes

```text
bac
acb
```

### Replaces

```text
bbc
cbc
...
```

### Inserts

```text
aabc
babc
...
```

This produces many possibilities.

Then filter against a dictionary.

---

# 51. Why Frequency Matters

Suppose query:

```text
teh
```

Possible corrections:

```text
the
ten
tech
```

Edit distance alone may not tell us which is intended.

Frequency gives us:

```text
P("the") >> P("ten")
```

So:

```text
the
```

is much more likely.

This leads naturally to the **noisy-channel model**.

---

# 52. Noisy-Channel Model

This is a more principled way of thinking about spelling correction.

Suppose the user typed:

```text
teh
```

We want the intended word:

```text
the
```

We want:

[
\arg\max_c P(c|w)
]

where:

* `w` = observed word
* `c` = intended/correct word

Using Bayes:

[
P(c|w)
\propto
P(c)P(w|c)
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\hat c = \arg\max_c P(c)P(w|c)
}
]

This is the equation you quoted.

---

# 53. What Does (P(c)) Mean?

[
P(c)
]

is the probability that `c` is the intended word.

Usually estimated from corpus frequency.

For example:

```text
the     5%
ten     0.1%
tech    0.05%
```

So:

```text
P(the)
```

is much larger.

This is called the:

> **language model / prior probability**

---

# 54. What Does (P(w|c)) Mean?

This means:

> Probability that the user would type `w` if they intended `c`.

For:

```text
c = the
w = teh
```

we might have:

[
P(teh|the)
]

which is relatively high because:

```text
the → teh
```

is a common transposition.

But:

```text
the → xyz
```

would have an extremely low probability.

This is the:

> **error model / channel model**

---

# 55. Noisy Channel Example

Suppose candidates:

```text
the
ten
tech
```

Observed:

```text
teh
```

Imagine:

| Candidate |   P(c) | P(teh|c) |
| --------- | -----: | -------: |
| the       |   0.05 |     0.01 |
| ten       |  0.001 |   0.0001 |
| tech      | 0.0005 |  0.00001 |

Calculate:

```text
the:
0.05 × 0.01 = 0.0005

ten:
0.001 × 0.0001 = 0.0000001

tech:
0.0005 × 0.00001 = 0.000000005
```

Therefore:

```text
the
```

wins.

---

# 56. Norvig vs Noisy Channel

These ideas are related but not identical.

### Norvig

Simplified:

```text
generate candidates
        ↓
filter dictionary
        ↓
choose highest frequency
```

Essentially:

[
\arg\max_c P(c)
]

among plausible edit-distance candidates.

### Noisy channel

More sophisticated:

```text
generate candidates
        ↓
language probability
        +
typing/error probability
        ↓
best candidate
```

[
\arg\max_c P(c)P(w|c)
]

So noisy channel accounts for **both word frequency and error likelihood**.

---

# 57. How All These Fit Together

This is the most important overall picture.

There are actually **three different problems**:

### Problem 1 — Measure similarity

```text
"kitten"
"sitting"

       ↓

Levenshtein = 3
```

Algorithms:

```text
Levenshtein
Damerau-Levenshtein
Hamming
Jaro
Jaro-Winkler
```

---

### Problem 2 — Align sequences

```text
sequence A
     ↕
sequence B
```

Algorithms:

```text
Needleman-Wunsch → global
Smith-Waterman  → local
```

---

### Problem 3 — Search a huge dictionary efficiently

```text
query
  ↓
millions of dictionary words
  ↓
find words within distance k
```

Techniques:

```text
Ukkonen / banded DP
Levenshtein automata
FST intersection
BK-trees
SymSpell
```

